# 4. Cross-Domain Invariant

Across all domains:

[
\chi > 1 \Rightarrow \text{Positive feedback dominates}
]

Choke points share three properties:

1. High flow concentration
2. Finite correction bandwidth
3. Nonlinear redistribution under overload

Collapse is therefore a phase transition triggered by ratio imbalance.

---

# 5. Detector Kernel (Operational Form)

For each node:

```
s_dot = weighted stress accumulation
D     = weighted dissipation bandwidth
chi   = s_dot / (D + epsilon)
```

Thresholds:

* Green: χ < 0.7
* Amber: 0.7 ≤ χ < 1.0
* Red: χ ≥ 1.0

This defines operational boundaries for intervention.

---

# 6. Forward Invariance Condition

Define safe set:

[
h(x) = 1 - \chi(x) \ge 0
]

Discrete-time barrier constraint:

[
h(x_{k+1}) \ge (1 - \eta) h(x_k)
]

This ensures system state remains forward-invariant under control action.

Energy density cannot scale faster than dissipation bandwidth.

---

# 7. Network Propagation Model

In graph-coupled systems:

[
\chi_i(t+1) = f\left(\chi_i(t), \sum_j w_{ij} \chi_j(t)\right)
]

If overload at node ( i ) redistributes stress to neighbors:

[
\chi_i > 1 \Rightarrow \dot{S}_j \uparrow \Rightarrow \chi_j \uparrow
]

Cascade occurs when spectral amplification exceeds damping.

---

# 8. Ω Synthesis

Define net coherence flow:

[
\Omega_i = D_i - \dot{S}_i
]

Then:

* Ω > 0 → stable regime
* Ω = 0 → critical surface
* Ω < 0 → runaway regime

The Choke Index is a normalized detector of the Ω sign.

---

# 9. Structural Conclusion

Choke points are density singularities created when injection bandwidth scales faster than correction bandwidth.

Stability is not achieved by eliminating entropy.

Stability requires ensuring dissipation capacity scales at least proportionally to injection rate.

This principle applies to:

* Thermal compute systems
* Electrical transmission networks
* Logistics throughput systems
* Financial liquidity systems
* Urban thermal ecosystems

The Universal Choke Equation therefore functions as a domain-agnostic instability detector rooted in rate imbalance and finite correction bandwidth.

